# Research: TLS MITM HTTP Proxy Libraries for Node.js/Bun

**Date**: 2026-04-07 | **Researcher**: nw-researcher (Nova) | **Confidence**: Medium-High | **Sources**: 12

## Executive Summary

Six Node.js libraries were evaluated for TLS MITM HTTP proxying with automatic CA and per-domain certificate generation: **mockttp**, **http-mitm-proxy** (+ @bjowes fork), **@pureproxy/mitmproxy**, **proxy-chain**, and **@lemonce/mitm**. Of these, **mockttp** (by HTTP Toolkit) is the clear leader -- it is actively maintained (v4.0.0, June 2025), TypeScript-native, battle-tested as the engine behind HTTP Toolkit, uses modern `@peculiar/x509` for certificate generation (replacing the unmaintained node-forge), and provides a clean embeddable API with request/response hooks ideal for policy evaluation.

**http-mitm-proxy** is the second-best option -- simpler, lighter-weight, and has a longer track record, but uses the aging node-forge for certificate generation and has not had a meaningful release in over 2 years on the original package. The @bjowes fork is more current (v0.9.6, January 2025).

A critical Bun compatibility constraint was discovered: **Bun did not support HTTP CONNECT** (required for HTTPS proxy tunneling) until a partial fix was merged in September 2025 (oven-sh/bun#16372). All MITM proxy libraries depend on `node:http`/`node:net`/`node:tls` -- they will work on Bun only if Bun's Node.js compatibility layer handles the CONNECT event and raw socket upgrade correctly. This must be validated empirically for whichever library is chosen.

**Recommendation**: Use **mockttp** as the primary candidate. It has the best maintenance, TypeScript types, modern crypto, and the richest hook API. Fall back to **http-mitm-proxy** (@bjowes fork) if mockttp's dependency footprint (express, connect, graphql-http, etc.) is too heavy for the embedded use case.

## Research Methodology
**Search Strategy**: npm registry keyword search (mitm, man-in-the-middle, https-intercept, ssl-proxy), GitHub repository analysis, HTTP Toolkit blog, Bun issue tracker, unpkg/raw.githubusercontent.com for package.json inspection
**Source Selection**: Types: GitHub repos (High), npm registry (High), official docs (High), technical blogs (Medium-High) | Verification: cross-referencing npm + GitHub + Bun issues
**Quality Standards**: Target 3 sources/claim (min 1 authoritative) | All major claims cross-referenced

## Findings

### Finding 1: Library Landscape

Six libraries were identified that provide some form of TLS MITM or HTTP proxy interception in Node.js:

| Library | npm Package | GitHub Stars | Purpose |
|---------|------------|-------------|---------|
| mockttp | `mockttp` | 853 | Full MITM proxy + HTTP mock server |
| http-mitm-proxy (original) | `http-mitm-proxy` | 703 | Dedicated MITM proxy |
| http-mitm-proxy (@bjowes fork) | `@bjowes/http-mitm-proxy` | -- (fork) | Maintained fork of above |
| proxy-chain | `proxy-chain` | 985 | HTTP/SOCKS proxy (tunneling only, NO MITM) |
| @pureproxy/mitmproxy | `@pureproxy/mitmproxy` | 7 | Minimal streaming MITM proxy |
| @lemonce/mitm | `@produck/mitm` | 6 | HTTP/HTTPS/WS MITM proxy |

**proxy-chain is NOT a MITM proxy** -- it only tunnels HTTPS via HTTP CONNECT without TLS termination. It cannot inspect or modify HTTPS request content. Eliminated from further analysis for this use case.

**Evidence**: GitHub READMEs, npm package pages
**Confidence**: High
**Verification**: [GitHub: joeferner/node-http-mitm-proxy](https://github.com/joeferner/node-http-mitm-proxy), [GitHub: httptoolkit/mockttp](https://github.com/httptoolkit/mockttp), [GitHub: apify/proxy-chain](https://github.com/apify/proxy-chain), [npm: @pureproxy/mitmproxy](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@pureproxy/mitmproxy)

### Finding 2: CA Generation and Per-Domain Certificate Features

#### mockttp

Uses `@peculiar/x509` and `@peculiar/asn1-*` libraries (WebCrypto-based) for certificate generation. Migrated from node-forge to produce certificates that "better match modern browser CA certificate standards." Keys are in PKCS#8 format.

**Programmatic CA generation**:
```typescript
import * as mockttp from 'mockttp';

// Generate a new CA certificate (async, in-memory)
const https = await mockttp.generateCACertificate();

// Create a proxy server with automatic per-domain cert generation
const server = mockttp.getLocal({ https });
await server.start(8080);

// Save CA for trust store installation
const { key, cert } = https;
fs.writeFileSync('ca-key.pem', key);
fs.writeFileSync('ca-cert.pem', cert);

// Or reuse saved CA across restarts
const server2 = mockttp.getLocal({
  https: { keyPath: './ca-key.pem', certPath: './ca-cert.pem' }
});
```

Per-domain certificates are generated transparently -- mockttp reads the TLS ClientHello SNI extension (via `read-tls-client-hello` dependency) and generates a certificate for that domain signed by the CA.

**Evidence**: [HTTP Toolkit blog](https://httptoolkit.com/blog/javascript-mitm-proxy-mockttp/), [GitHub: mockttp releases](https://github.com/httptoolkit/mockttp/releases), [mockttp package.json](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/httptoolkit/mockttp/main/package.json)
**Confidence**: High
**Verification**: Blog post, GitHub release notes, package.json dependency list all confirm @peculiar/x509 migration

#### http-mitm-proxy (original + @bjowes fork)

Uses **node-forge** (pure JS TLS/crypto library) for certificate generation. Auto-generates a CA on first run, saves to disk at `options.sslCaDir + '/certs/ca.pem'`. Per-domain certs are generated on demand and cached to disk.

**Programmatic usage**:
```javascript
const Proxy = require('http-mitm-proxy');
const proxy = new Proxy();

proxy.onError(function(ctx, err) { console.error(err); });

proxy.onRequest(function(ctx, callback) {
  // ctx.clientToProxyRequest -- the incoming request
  // ctx.proxyToServerRequest -- outgoing to upstream (after connect)
  console.log('Request:', ctx.clientToProxyRequest.url);
  callback();
});

proxy.onResponse(function(ctx, callback) {
  // ctx.serverToProxyResponse -- upstream response
  callback();
});

// Optional: override certificate resolution per-host
proxy.onCertificateRequired = function(hostname, callback) {
  callback(null, {
    keyFile: path.resolve('/ca/certs/', hostname + '.key'),
    certFile: path.resolve('/ca/certs/', hostname + '.pem')
  });
};

proxy.listen({ port: 8080, sslCaDir: './ca' });
// CA cert at ./ca/certs/ca.pem after first run
```

**Evidence**: [GitHub: joeferner/node-http-mitm-proxy README](https://github.com/joeferner/node-http-mitm-proxy), [npm: http-mitm-proxy](https://www.npmjs.com/package/http-mitm-proxy)
**Confidence**: High

#### @pureproxy/mitmproxy

Provides two certificate manager classes: `CertManager` (in-memory) and `CertManagerFs` (persistent to disk). Extends a base `ProxyServer` class; interception controlled by `shouldIntercept()` method.

**Evidence**: [GitHub: pureproxy/mitmproxy](https://github.com/pureproxy/mitmproxy)
**Confidence**: Medium (low star count, limited documentation)

### Finding 3: Maintenance Status (2024-2025)

| Library | Latest Version | Last Publish | Activity Level |
|---------|---------------|-------------|----------------|
| mockttp | 4.0.0 (June 2025) | June 2025 | **Active** -- regular releases, TypeScript, used by HTTP Toolkit product |
| http-mitm-proxy (original) | 1.1.0 | ~2023 | **Stale** -- 87 open issues, no recent releases |
| @bjowes/http-mitm-proxy | 0.9.6 | Jan 2025 | **Maintained** -- security/bug fixes only, single maintainer for cypress-ntlm-auth |
| @pureproxy/mitmproxy | -- | June 2023 | **Dormant** -- 7 stars, 25 total commits |
| @lemonce/mitm | -- | Unknown | **Dormant** -- 6 stars |
| proxy-chain | -- | Dec 2024 | **Active** -- but not MITM capable |

**Analysis**: Only mockttp has active feature development and a commercial product backing it (HTTP Toolkit). The @bjowes fork of http-mitm-proxy receives maintenance patches but no new features.

**Evidence**: npm publish dates, GitHub commit histories
**Confidence**: High
**Verification**: [npm: @bjowes/http-mitm-proxy](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@bjowes/http-mitm-proxy), [GitHub: httptoolkit/mockttp releases](https://github.com/httptoolkit/mockttp/releases)

### Finding 4: Bun Compatibility

**Critical constraint: HTTP CONNECT support**

All TLS MITM proxies work via HTTP CONNECT tunneling:
1. Client sends `CONNECT example.com:443` to proxy
2. Proxy accepts, establishes a raw TCP tunnel
3. Proxy performs TLS handshake with client (using generated cert)
4. Proxy reads plaintext HTTP, evaluates policy, forwards upstream

Bun did **not** support the HTTP CONNECT method until September 2025. The fix (oven-sh/bun#22756) was merged with the note: "This is a partial implementation with known limitations around raw socket handling."

**Timeline**:
- Issue filed: oven-sh/bun#16372 (pre-2025)
- Duplicate filed: oven-sh/bun#18583 (March 2025, tested Bun 1.2.5-1.2.7, all broken)
- Fix merged: September 19, 2025 (PR #22756) -- partial, with raw socket limitations
- Status as of April 2026: fix is in released Bun versions, but "known limitations around raw socket handling" may affect MITM proxy libraries

**Bun's `node:tls` support**: Bun wraps OpenSSL and supports `tls.createServer`, `tls.connect`, `tls.createSecureContext`, certificate parsing, and session resumption. This is sufficient for the TLS handshake portion of MITM.

**Bun's `node:net` support**: Needed for raw TCP socket handling during CONNECT tunnel setup. The "known limitations" warning in the CONNECT fix suggests this may be incomplete.

**node-forge vs @peculiar/x509**: node-forge is pure JavaScript (no native dependencies), so it works anywhere. The `@peculiar/x509` stack used by mockttp relies on WebCrypto (`@peculiar/webcrypto` on Node.js), which Bun supports via its Web API compatibility.

**Practical assessment**: None of the libraries explicitly claim Bun support. All depend on `node:http`, `node:net`, and `node:tls` from Node.js. Whether they work on Bun depends entirely on Bun's Node.js compatibility layer fidelity, specifically:
1. CONNECT event emission from `http.createServer` (fixed with limitations)
2. Raw socket upgrade after CONNECT (the "known limitations" area)
3. `tls.createServer` / `tls.createSecureContext` (supported)

**Evidence**: [Bun issue #18583](https://github.com/oven-sh/bun/issues/18583), [Bun issue #16372](https://github.com/oven-sh/bun/issues/16372), [Bun TLS docs](https://bun.com/reference/node/tls)
**Confidence**: Medium -- the CONNECT fix exists but "known limitations" are not documented in detail. **Empirical validation required.**

### Finding 5: Embeddability and Programmatic API

#### mockttp -- Best API for policy evaluation hooks

```typescript
import * as mockttp from 'mockttp';

const https = await mockttp.generateCACertificate();
const server = mockttp.getLocal({ https });

// Rule: intercept all requests, run policy evaluation
server.forAnyRequest().thenCallback(async (request) => {
  const { method, url, headers, body } = request;

  // Policy evaluation hook
  const decision = await evaluatePolicy({ method, url, headers, body });

  if (decision === 'deny') {
    return { statusCode: 403, body: 'Blocked by policy' };
  }

  // Forward to upstream (passthrough)
  return 'passthrough';
});

// Rule: passthrough with response inspection
server.forAnyRequest().thenPassThrough({
